+# 回归报告 / Regression Report: single-generation reply and emotion
+
+## Change and necessity
+
+The offline main brain now emits a bounded `[neko_emotion:happy]` header and its normal spoken reply in the same generation. A request-local stream filter separates the header before text validation, native tool history, UI, TTS and conversation history. The frontend applies the supported emotion immediately through the existing expression/motion mapper, and completion does not submit a classifier request for these turns.
+
+Previously, each finished reply could submit a second inference request to `/api/emotion/analysis`. On a shared local GPU that added input work and queue contention. This change removes that optional request for offline main replies, including callback delivery and buffered Phase 2 proactive replies. It does not merge later world observations or real tool feedback into an earlier generation.
+
+## Compatibility and fallbacks
+
+- The offline constructor remains opt-in; the core main brain opts in. Realtime sessions retain their existing protocol.
+- Supported labels are neutral, happy, sad, angry and surprised. Missing, invalid or truncated metadata uses neutral without a retry/classifier request. Only a reserved leading header is interpreted; literal examples in the body remain text.
+- Native tool arguments keep their original schema. Tool responses still require subsequent generations when they contain new evidence.
+- Mutable metadata belongs to each coroutine through a ContextVar, with cleanup on cancellation. It is not owned by a shared client field or an async generator finalizer.
+- Phase 2 keeps its routing prefix and PASS semantics, strips the body header before dedup/data guards and TTS, and carries metadata into the existing guarded commit. Its regeneration path refreshes the emotion from the replacement reply.
+- Logs contain only turn ID, source and canonical emotion, never reply/prompt contents.
+
+## Potential regressions and validation
+
+Chunked headers, retained tool rounds, cancellation, proactive PASS, literal body references, missing labels and stale frontend completions are the critical boundaries. Targeted coverage runs the real offline tool loop for ordinary and ephemeral turns, verifies clean history and unchanged tool arguments, and rejects stale/cancelled avatar updates. The frontend keeps legacy classifier coalescing for non-opted sessions.
+
+Validation on 2026-10-11: 395 relevant Python tests, 19 frontend tests, JavaScript syntax and Ruff checks passed. Existing mock coroutine/deprecation warnings remain. A protocol replay proves one provider request for a normal reply and zero frontend classifier requests; a tool-feedback turn still uses its necessary second request. This is not an aggregate latency or subjective expression-quality claim. Live deployment evidence is recorded separately in the local runtime directory.
